From 156f103788ea71a7a6304141a38d3665a378fb64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 14 Jul 2026 01:38:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07005/11362] Fix SGE anti-stagnation partial-escape gap:
 forced portfolio now rotates instead of re-selecting the same signature
 (REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-2)

exp5699 disclosed a real, honest limitation: once collapse fires and escapes
the LLM's own repeated strategy, the deterministic forced portfolio could
settle into a NEW static repetition of its own -- three of the five
categories (observation, action_type_probe, recovery_reset) intentionally
tolerate re-selecting a past-failed signature, but nothing prevented them
from picking the exact same top-ranked candidate every subsequent call on a
frozen candidate pool. A real 90-budget live run measured the identical
5-candidate selection on 44 consecutive forced-portfolio calls.

Fix: AntiStagnationDiversityController now tracks
_recently_forced_signatures(history, window=5) -- every signature ANY
forced-portfolio category selected recently, regardless of outcome (a
forced-portfolio row is appended with outcome="pending" and typically never
gets a real outcome before the next call). rank_forced_portfolio's
add_from_pool makes two passes per category: pass 1 excludes recently-forced
signatures (rotate to the next-best candidate); pass 2, entered only when
pass 1 finds nothing in an otherwise non-empty pool, falls back to
re-selecting so the pre-existing category-fill guarantee isn't weakened.
The router's history-append now records a forced_signatures list (every
category's pick, not just the first/observation one, which is all the
pre-existing singular chosen_signature field ever captured) so the rotation
tracker sees the full picture.

Verified two ways, deliberately not conflated:
- Unit-level: a 20-candidate pool (vs max_candidates=8) shows genuine
  rotation across consecutive calls; a 1-candidate pool correctly falls
  back to re-selecting and reports rotation_exhausted_categories.
- Live-level, an honest non-bug finding: re-running the exact g50t scenario
  that exposed the bug still showed static repetition after this fix.
  Investigation (no GPU needed) found the real cause: g50t's spawn frame
  has EXACTLY 5 candidates (keyboard ACTION1-5, no click action 6, zero
  coordinate diversity) -- matching the controller's 5 categories exactly,
  so every pool is exhausted the first time it fills. No algorithm can
  rotate when there's nothing left to rotate to; this is a structural
  property of g50t's candidate space, not a defect in the fix. sk48 (45
  candidates at spawn) was tried as a richer live target but didn't trigger
  collapse within budget -- inconclusive, not negative.

5 new tests (40 total in the file, 100% coverage on the module).
REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-2 + SCENARIO-ARC-FCP-5699-2-FORCED-PORTFOLIO-ROTATES added
to spec.md.

